prompt: Load the `writing-style` skill from ~/.claude/skills/writing-style/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/writing-style/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[writing-style] applies` on its own line. Then answer the following in 4–8 sentences. This is a dry-run description task, not a live teach: do NOT call any file-editing tool and do NOT modify the skill file — describe only. (a) I say `new rule: prefer "to" over "in order to"` — show the exact `## Learned rules` entry you would append and confirm what you'd do. (b) Separately, I say `I prefer shorter sentences` — what do you do with that, and why?
expect_substr: [writing-style] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer distinguishes the explicit-teach trigger from a conversational non-trigger, per the skill's Learning loop section. (1) For `new rule: …` — recognises it as one of the strict prefix triggers (`new rule:` / `add rule:` / `style rule:`) and shows an entry it would APPEND to the `## Learned rules` section, newest at top, in the skill's shape: a date-stamped line carrying the rule text, e.g. "- <date> · prefer \"to\" over \"in order to\"" optionally with `· *example:*` and `· source: explicit teach` fields. It should note the append lands under `## Learned rules` (not silently edited into a body section — promotion into Voice/Vocabulary/etc. is a separate, later step) and that it confirms with a short line afterward. (2) For "I prefer shorter sentences" — recognises this is NOT a trigger (it lacks the `new rule:`/`add rule:`/`style rule:` prefix), so it does NOT capture a rule; it treats the remark conversationally to avoid false-positive captures. Wrong answers to flag: treating "I prefer…" or "stop using…" as a capture trigger; writing the new rule directly into a body section instead of appending to `## Learned rules`; omitting the date stamp or the newest-at-top ordering; claiming it would edit a file when the prompt said not to.
tools: claude,kiro,codex
